- Authority: what a task may do. It only narrows when a task delegates.\x0A- Connectors: outside systems, such as the forge.\x0A\x0ASpend comes from the project's weekly budget; a delegate's is carved from its parent's. A task is held when it needs a decision: its budget, deadline or tries ran out; an effect failed or its outcome is uncertain; the forge changed under its work; its executor stalled; a deployment limit was reached; or a person stopped it. temper never guesses past a hold: the task above it or a person releases it.\x0A\x0AA chat talks with its person. A goal is tracked and prioritized: it plans, delegates changes and keeps a report. A change carries work to landing: an agent produces it on a branch, then a procedure carries it on. The landing rules of its branch say what it must pass: CI, being up to date, gates (an agent's review, or a test workflow) and approvals by role. Stock: produce (an agent); CI (code); review (an agent); repair what either found (an agent); then queue by priority, update and merge (code), with conflicts resolved by an agent. A project may land by turn, keeping gate results per tree, and add more, such as suites after landing. No agent holds a merge or update tool, and a merge lands exactly the commit its checks saw. A person comes in where the settings say: an approval, a question passed up to them, a held task's release.\x0A\x0ASettings define how work runs: charters (each role's instructions, models and tools); templates (how chats and goals start); policies (roles and what each may do and spend); landing rules; project landing (by queue or turn) and repository test gates; forge charters (which roles produce and review); and project ways and questions (what its pages offer and where change questions go). project-classes: what agents decide for a person and what asks them, by kind and by path set. Owners use Settings or accept proposals. Every change, and each release's new defaults, makes a numbered revision. Runs read their